able, but I just wanted to note that here. Ultimately, we also have to have the hearing and the two readings before council that I mentioned. These dates have not been set in stone yet, but we have considered that they would occur probably in the end of October or during the month of December. For the most part tonight, WE'RE GOING TO TALK ABOUT DATA CENTERS IN GENERAL AND HOW THIS ZONING ORDINANCE WOULD IMPACT DATA CENTERS THROUGHOUT THE CITY. HOWEVER, WE ALL KNOW THAT TWO DATA CENTERS HAVE BEEN PROPOSED IN THE CITY AT THIS TIME. ONE IS ON GREENFIELD ROAD AND ONE IS ON HARRISBURG PIKE. I THINK IT'S IMPORTANT TO TALK ABOUT THE ZONING MAP AND MAYBE TO A CERTAIN EXTENT the comprehensive plan future land use map as well the um the location or the data center that has been proposed at the greenfield road location is in the suburban manufacturing zoning district the map on the left you can see it's in the top right corner in the blue color and then the harrisburg pike location is in that lavender color it's in the central manufacturing zoning district and it's sort of in the top left, fairly close to Longs Park. So one of the things I just wanted to say in general here, I think there may be more questions about this, but it is not lawful for us as a municipality to exclude any particular land use in the city, including data centers. Our comp plan and our zoning ordinance determines where specific uses are best suited. So I know there has been a lot of consternation about the zoning determination related to the Greenfield Road site, for example. I know there's some granular issues there at play, but just wanted to start with the suburban manufacturing zoning district itself. I want to read you the definition from that particular zoning district in our zoning code. Okay. So again, this is suburban manufacturing. not a lot of uh this district does not cover a lot of land in the city but this is where the 216 greenfield road is located the suburban manufacturing district is 